/*! \page IO_input Input workbook

All physics parameters are in one workbook, radcluster_code/input/input_parameters.xlsx. The class \ref py_utils::input_data::InputData "InputData" reads it and computes the derived quantities.

The workbook has five sheets:

| Sheet | Content |
|---|---|
| Production | survival efficiency, displacement and helium generation rates, clustered fractions, cascade power-law slopes and cut-offs. One column per spectrum (fission and fusion) |
| Energetics | lattice and elastic constants, atomic volume, formation energies, surface energy, loop Burgers vectors |
| Diffusion | pre-exponentials and migration energies for vacancies, SIAs and helium, 1D-glide parameters, solute concentrations and trap binding energies, mobility cut-offs |
| Dissociation | binding-energy models: void capillarity, helium virial equation of state, loop binding fits, trap-mutation barriers |
| Reactions | geometric prefactors, dislocation bias factors, sink densities, cluster-size limits, the discrete/bin split, solver settings |

Each row of a sheet contains:
- a parameter name
- a Symbol key
- a value
- the units
- a note that cites the equation or the table the value comes from

\section IO_input_overrides Overrides

The Symbol key is what a run-time override uses:

\verbatim
PARAM_OVERRIDES = {'T': 673.0, 'rho_d': 5e14, 'eta': 0.28}
\endverbatim

An override replaces the workbook value for that run only, and it is recorded in the provenance file of the run (see \ref IO_output). Permanent changes belong in the workbook.

\b Note that physics lives in the workbook and not in the code. The code reads the parameters. It does not hard-code them.

\sa \ref IO_output, \ref Materials_eurofer97

*/
